Deliberately ahead of upstream in this copy:

  * README.md carries a vendoring note at the top. It describes THIS copy and
    would be false upstream, so it stays here and is re-applied on every
    re-vendor.

  * lib/git.php has a four-line comment on the DOCI_DOMAIN check in
    git_commit_and_push that references documents.sh writing "localhost" on
    no-domain installs. Upstream's shorter comment says
    DOCI_DOMAIN is required "no silent fallback to doci.local", which is true
    but does not name the smart-node companion behaviour. The runtime check
    itself is identical to upstream. Push this comment upstream on next
    opportunity so this drift retires.

  * The folder examples in api/list.php, mcp_server.py and
    docs/05-api-reference.md use a neutral project name. The source guard
    tests/core-business-agnostic.test.sh rejects business names if a later
    re-vendor restores the upstream examples.

Deliberately not vendored: files/, the document store, which documents.sh
creates on the node via ensure_files_store.

lib/vendor IS vendored and must stay. It is not composer output -- composer.json
requires only PHP extensions -- it is Parsedown.php and ParsedownExtended.php
placed there by hand, and lib/markdown.php loads them. .dockerignore drops a
top-level vendor/ only, so this path does reach the image. Dropping it silently
removes markdown rendering. Note that companions/doci/.gitignore line 25 says
vendor/, which matches this path, so those two files have to be added with
git add -f. Upstream tracks them the same way. The previous vendored copy
side-stepped this by keeping Parsedown at the tree root, which is why the
re-vendor shows them moving.

Why this marker exists at all: doci was the ONE vendored companion without it,
so tools/vendor-drift.sh did not watch it, and it fell five months behind
without anything noticing. The copy shipped as 0.2.0-rc while Office called
/api/list.php, an endpoint upstream added in 2b56f93 and this tree did not
have, so the documents page rendered empty on every node built from a release.
Reported from a node in the field against 1.12.33.

Two changes that lived only here were pushed upstream before this re-vendor
rather than being carried forward again: the handler-set HTTP status fix
(6f4a4f9) and Dockerfile.mcp (aefeb98). Anything found ahead in future goes the
same way -- upstream first, then re-vendor.
